\begin{proof}
We consider two cases.

First, if $p=q$, then $|G|=p^2$. A group of order $p^2$ is abelian, and any subgroup of order $p$ is therefore normal. Such a subgroup is nontrivial and proper, so $G$ is not simple.

Second, suppose $p\ne q$. Without loss of generality, let $p<q$. Let $n_q$ be the number of Sylow $q$-subgroups of $G$. Sylow's theorems imply that $n_q$ divides $p$ and that $n_q\equiv1\pmod q$. Hence $n_q\in\{1,p\}$. Because $p<q$, the value $p$ is not congruent to $1$ modulo $q$, so $n_q=1$. The unique Sylow $q$-subgroup is consequently normal in $G$, and it is a nontrivial proper subgroup. Therefore $G$ is not simple.
\end{proof}
